[davel@cbnewsl.att.com (David Loewenstern) writes:]
L] competitive nethacking already cheat by copying save files.  Competitive
L] nethack would be fun for the rest of us, and probably doesn't open more
L] roads to cheating than already exist.

fun for _some_ of us, perhaps ...

i get a sense that "competitive" nethacking is just another artificial
constraint towards ranking the game.  as i said, that may be fine for
some (whatever floats your boat).

there is some technical merit to forcing a dungeon but it hardly seems
like _my_ idea of fair play.  you may not agree.

[hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:]
H] is there an overwhelming reason the dungeons have to be identical
H] to be compared?  there is enough randomness in the game that one
H] could argue that advantages and disadvantages "average" out over
H] the course of a game.

L] ...You're joking right?  You get the same scores regardless of the 
L] number or location of shops and altars?  Your wizards do just as well
L] whether they start with a ring of conflict or a ring of adornment?

one could argue that the better player is the one who can get off of
level 1 the fastest with the highest score.  of course, this would be
a whimsical criterion of "better".  averaging this out over a game
seems more natural, but its still arbitrary.  the requirement that
the dungeons be identical (to the extent possible) is an attempt to
appease this arbitrary sense of what's better.  it implies that there
is mostly one correct way to play.  again, that's fine if that's what
you want from the game.

in my previous message i was assuming that you would average scores over
many games (after all, one game could be a fluke, no?).  this gets more
to the point of who's the "better" player as opposed to who could play
a particular game the best (in an arbitrary sense).  still there's the
super bowl and other arbitrary contests so it gets back to whatever
floats your boat.  if one accepts that averaging scores over many games
is a fairer criterion then the requirement that the dungeons each be
identical is not as important.

in all of this, keep in mind that fixing the random seed does have
technical merit (similar to wizard-mode) but hardly seems like my
idea of real play (on this point i'm a pessimist).

